VITA

Terence John Tarnowsky was born in Manhasset, New York on January 24, 1979. He graduated from Binghamton University (State University of New York at Binghamton) where he obtained a Bachelor of Science degree in Physics with a double major in Chemistry. He also worked as an undergraduate teaching assistant and research assistant from 1999-2001 for the Physics Department at Binghamton University. From 2001-2002 he served as a graduate teaching assistant for the Physics Department at Purdue University. From 2002-2008 he worked as a graduate research assistant in high-energy experimental physics. He was a member of the STAR experiment from 2003-2008 and contributed to the calibration and operation of a detector subsystem. He received his Ph.D. in experimental high energy nuclear physics from Purdue University in May 2008. His dissertation is a significant contribution to understanding the system produced in high energy heavy ion collisions and is the first measurement of forward-backward long-range multiplicity correlations in heavy ion collisions as a function of centrality, indicating multiple partonic interactions. The title of his dissertation is "Long-Range Multiplicity Correlations in Relativistic Heavy Ion Collisions as a Signal for Dense Partonic Matter"